From 56bcd7e673959facfdc6d15e99b890b610354f6a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HiGarfield <32226909+HiGarfield@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 04:56:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] usb: xhci-mtk: set EP_BPKTS for SS bulk and control endpoints

The MTK controller's internal hardware scheduler requires a non-zero
EP_BPKTS value in the endpoint context reserved fields for every
SuperSpeed endpoint, not just periodic ones.

When a SS hub is attached, its interrupt (status-change) endpoint goes
through xhci_mtk_add_ep_quirk() and activates the port's bandwidth
scheduler.  Once active, the scheduler enforces EP_BPKTS for *all* SS
endpoints on the same root port.  Bulk and control endpoints skip the
bandwidth scheduling path (need_bw_sch() returns false for them) and
therefore leave EP_BPKTS at the zero-initialised default.  The hardware
treats EP_BPKTS == 0 as "zero packets to transfer" and stops
scheduling transfers for those endpoints, causing COMP_TX_ERR errors
under high load (e.g. sustained reads from a USB3 hard drive behind a
USB3 hub).  This surfaces as repeated "reset SuperSpeed USB device"
messages and eventually as disk disconnects.

A directly-attached disk has no periodic endpoints, so the scheduler
never activates and the hardware falls back to its default bulk
scheduling, which is why the same setup without a hub is stable.

Fix this by setting EP_BPKTS = bMaxBurst + 1 and EP_BBM = 1 for all
SS non-periodic endpoints whenever they are added.  No bandwidth
reservation is needed for bulk/control, so the full check_sch_bw()
path is still bypassed; only the hardware-mandatory scheduling fields
are written.

Co-authored-by: Copilot <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/usb/host/xhci-mtk-sch.c | 25 ++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/usb/host/xhci-mtk-sch.c b/drivers/usb/host/xhci-mtk-sch.c
index d6f6f33c..3544a92f 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/host/xhci-mtk-sch.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/host/xhci-mtk-sch.c
@@ -361,8 +361,31 @@ int xhci_mtk_add_ep_quirk(struct usb_hcd *hcd, struct usb_device *udev,
 		GET_MAX_PACKET(usb_endpoint_maxp(&ep->desc)),
 		usb_endpoint_dir_in(&ep->desc), ep);
 
-	if (!need_bw_sch(ep, udev->speed, slot_ctx->tt_info & TT_SLOT))
+	if (!need_bw_sch(ep, udev->speed, slot_ctx->tt_info & TT_SLOT)) {
+		/*
+		 * On SS, the MTK controller's internal scheduler requires
+		 * a non-zero EP_BPKTS value for every endpoint, including
+		 * bulk and control.  When a periodic endpoint (e.g. a SS
+		 * hub's status interrupt endpoint) is present on the same
+		 * root port, the scheduler becomes active for that port and
+		 * treats EP_BPKTS == 0 as "zero packets to transfer",
+		 * effectively blocking the endpoint.  Under high load this
+		 * manifests as COMP_TX_ERR on the bulk endpoint of a hard
+		 * drive attached behind a SS hub, leading to frequent device
+		 * resets.  Set the minimum required scheduling fields so the
+		 * controller can always schedule these endpoints correctly.
+		 */
+		if (udev->speed == USB_SPEED_SUPER &&
+		    !usb_endpoint_xfer_isoc(&ep->desc) &&
+		    !usb_endpoint_xfer_int(&ep->desc)) {
+			u32 max_burst =
+				CTX_TO_MAX_BURST(le32_to_cpu(ep_ctx->ep_info2));
+			ep_ctx->reserved[0] = cpu_to_le32(
+				EP_BPKTS(max_burst + 1) | EP_BBM(1));
+			ep_ctx->reserved[1] = 0;
+		}
 		return 0;
+	}
 
 	bw_index = get_bw_index(xhci, udev, ep);
 	sch_bw = &sch_array[bw_index];
-- 
2.43.0

